(* ============================================================================
   RENDER-LOSS REPORTING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-024] (a stable id: it keeps its
   P2 prefix and `part` in `rules.json` though it stands here). Target routing may
   intentionally omit a raw node, flatten a `ruby` node or an editorial
   comment, or displace an authored attribute, but a checked
   renderer MUST make that loss observable. Its
   result has `value`, `losses`, `totalLosses`, and `truncated`. Each raw
   node the selected target does not render contributes one loss in document order with the stable code
   `raw-format-dropped`, its verbatim `format`, the selected `target`, a
   `nodeType` of `inline` or `block`, its source `pos` when recorded, and a
   short human-readable `message`. `losses` is bounded (100 entries by default),
   `totalLosses` counts the complete render, and `truncated` says whether the
   bounded array omitted entries.

   WHERE A LOSS POINTS. A loss in content an include brought in (PART 6)
   points the way a lint finding in included content does (PART 6 I16). It
   carries `file`, the included file's I11 identity, and its `pos` is counted
   in that file, so content selected by `@lines` or `@region` keeps its
   source coordinates. It also carries `includedBy`, the include chain with
   the top-level document's directive first, each entry the directive's
   `file`, `line` and `column`. A loss in the top-level document carries
   neither field, and `pos` never carries a file. A CLI summary line names
   the loss's own file and position, never the including file's name with
   the included file's line, for example
   `snippets/div.crv:2:1 markdown-container-flattened - Flattened a container
   into Markdown blocks (included from docs/d.crv:3:1)`.

   Each flattened `ruby` inline node contributes one loss in document order
   with the stable code `ruby-flattened`, the selected `target`, `nodeType` of
   `inline`, its `pos` when recorded, and a short message. It has NO `format`.
   Plain, ANSI and canonical Carve output keep both sides visibly as
   `base(annotation)` but lose their association. HTML and Markdown preserve
   native ruby and report no loss. On the canonical Carve target the same
   flatten is also reported as `structure-unspellable`, node `ruby`, on the
   conversion-diagnostics channel (CARVE-P11-046): one loss under two codes,
   one per channel.

   Each editorial comment that Plain or ANSI writes as bare text contributes
   one loss in document order with the stable code
   `editorial-comment-flattened`, the selected `target`, `nodeType` of
   `inline`, its `pos` when recorded, and NO `format`. Its `message` is NAMED
   HERE and emitted verbatim: `Flattened an editorial comment into the
   surrounding text`. HTML, Markdown and canonical Carve keep the comment and
   report no loss.

   Each destination the PART 9 §25 sink denylist blanks contributes one loss in
   document order with the stable code `destination-denied`, the selected
   `target`, `nodeType` of `inline`, its `pos` when recorded, and NO `format`.
   Its `message` is NAMED HERE and emitted verbatim: a link or autolink
   destination takes `Blanked a denied destination scheme`, and an image source
   takes `Blanked a denied image source`. `nodeType` is `inline` for both, so
   the message is where the sink kind survives; `target` is already a field, so
   the message does not repeat it.

   Each authored attribute roundtrip export drops under CARVE-P10-019
   contributes one loss in document order with the stable code
   `carrier-attribute-dropped`, the selected `target`, `nodeType` of `inline` or
   `block`, its `pos` when recorded, and NO `format`. Such an entry MUST carry
   `attribute`, the dropped attribute's name, because the name is the whole
   content of the finding and a message is not compared. Only the HTML target
   writes carriers, so only that target reports this code.

   Default Markdown export reports a flattened `div` as
   `markdown-container-flattened`, each block whose authored attributes have
   no Markdown representation as `markdown-attributes-dropped`, and each figure
   or table caption written as ordinary text as `markdown-caption-flattened`.
   This also applies to a table from a CSV code include: Markdown export writes
   the GFM pipe table and its caption as a paragraph, with
   `markdown-caption-flattened`, exactly as for the authored CSV table. It does
   not write the source `^ ` caption line literally.
   A promoted image (PART 9R R7) reports `markdown-attributes-dropped` only
   for an authored block attribute run merged into it, as in
   `{.outer k=one}` above `![a](b){.inner k=two}`. Attributes glued to the
   image follow the inline rule and report no loss, exactly as for the same
   image mid-paragraph: `![alt](img.png){.x}` alone on its line reports
   none. The loss follows the construct the author wrote, not how it
   renders. The shared AST keeps the merged set on the inline image, so an
   AST-only renderer sees no block run and reports none.
   A code fence title dropped because the fence has no language reports
   `fence-title-dropped` with message
   `Dropped a code fence title without a language in Markdown`.
   A source-to-Markdown checked entry point also reports an unattached block
   attribute run with `markdown-attributes-dropped`, since parsing discards it
   before the renderer receives a tree. An AST-only renderer has no such run.
   Escaped raw HTML contributes `raw-html-escaped` for a block or inline node.
   These records have `target` `markdown`, the source `pos` when recorded, and
   `nodeType` `block` except for raw HTML inlines. Roundtrip carriers preserve
   containers, attributes and captions, so those three codes are omitted when
   roundtrip export carries them. Escaped raw HTML still reports its loss.

   Each alphabetic or roman ordered list (`a.`, `A.`, `i.`, `I.`) the
   Markdown target writes contributes one loss in document order with the
   stable code `list-style-dropped`, the selected `target`, `nodeType` of
   `block`, its `pos` when recorded, NO `format`, and `style`, one of
   `lower-alpha`, `upper-alpha`, `lower-roman` or `upper-roman`. Markdown
   numbers in decimal only, so the list keeps its start and delimiter and
   loses its style. Under roundtrip export the list-style marker carries the
   style (CARVE-P11-067) and the list reports no loss. Only the Markdown
   target reports this code.

   An ordered list the Markdown target writes with a delimiter other than
   its authored one, to keep it apart from an adjacent list, contributes
   one loss with the stable code `list-delimiter-changed`; CARVE-P11-057
   names its fields.

   Each table cell with a `colspan` or `rowspan` greater than 1 that the
   Markdown, Plain or ANSI target writes contributes one loss in document
   order with the stable code `table-span-flattened`, the selected `target`,
   `nodeType` of `block`, the cell's `pos` when recorded, NO `format`, and
   `colspan`, `rowspan` or both: each present exactly when the cell's count is
   greater than 1, holding that count. Its `message` is NAMED HERE and emitted
   verbatim: `Flattened a spanning table cell into separate cells`. These
   targets have no span syntax, so the cell's content stays at its origin and
   every position it covers is written as an empty cell. HTML and canonical
   Carve keep the span and report no loss.

   Each Carve header row after the first that the Markdown target writes as
   an ordinary body row (CARVE-P11-031) contributes one loss in document order
   with the stable code `table-header-row-demoted`, the selected `target`,
   `nodeType` of `block`, the row's `pos` when recorded, and NO `format`. Its
   `message` is NAMED HERE and emitted verbatim: `Wrote a later header row as
   a body row`. A pipe table identifies one header row, so the row keeps its
   cells and loses its header semantics. Only the Markdown target reports
   this code.

   A first Carve header row that body rows precede, and that the Markdown
   target therefore writes as the pipe table's header above them
   (CARVE-P11-031), contributes one loss with the stable code
   `table-header-row-hoisted`, the selected `target`, `nodeType` of `block`,
   the row's `pos` when recorded, and NO `format`. Its `message` is NAMED
   HERE and emitted verbatim: `Moved a header row above earlier body rows`.
   The row keeps its cells and its header semantics, and the rows lose their
   authored order. Only the Markdown target reports this code.

   Each header cell in a row that is not a header row (CARVE-P11-056 defines
   one), which the Markdown target writes as an ordinary body cell,
   contributes one loss in document order with the stable code
   `table-header-cell-demoted`, the selected `target`, `nodeType` of `block`,
   the cell's `pos` when recorded, and NO `format`. Its `message` is NAMED
   HERE and emitted verbatim: `Wrote a header cell as a body cell`. A pipe
   table marks headers only by row, so the cell keeps its content and loses
   its header semantics. Only the Markdown target reports this code. A row's
   own loss precedes its cells' losses, and a cell's
   `table-header-cell-demoted` loss precedes its `table-span-flattened` loss.

   The canonical Carve writer contributes no `destination-denied` row
   (CARVE-P9-054).

   A LOSS RECORDS WHAT THE SELECTED TARGET DOES NOT RENDER, not what its
   output drops and not every raw or ruby node in the tree. A raw inline
   whose format the target cannot write is not rendered, so it reports
   `raw-format-dropped` whether or not roundtrip export carries its content
   in a `data-carve-raw` span (CARVE-P10-015): the carrier keeps the content
   for an import, and this output still does not render it.
   Matching HTML passes through (or is visibly escaped by a safe
   policy, [CARVE-P10-013]) with no loss. Markdown visibly escapes HTML raw content and reports `raw-html-escaped`.
   ANSI visibly degrades a raw BLOCK to `[raw:format] content` with no loss, but
   reports a raw INLINE it omits. Plain text reports every raw node it omits.
   The canonical Carve writer preserves raw nodes and reports none.

   A render-time refusal, such as a nested `::: footnotes` placement marker
   (CARVE-P9-073), MUST NOT appear in either machine-readable report; it is a
   lint finding, and `--strict-losses` does not fail on it.

   A strict checked render MUST finish collecting the complete bounded report,
   then fail without returning or publishing `value` when `totalLosses > 0`.
   Existing string-returning APIs MAY remain compatibility conveniences, but
   every binding MUST expose the checked result and strict form. A CLI MUST keep
   rendered stdout pure, summarize losses on stderr, fail with
   `--strict-losses`, offer a machine-readable JSON report, and permit the
   explicit exceptions `--allow-loss raw-format-dropped`,
   `--allow-loss ruby-flattened`,
   `--allow-loss editorial-comment-flattened`,
   `--allow-loss carrier-attribute-dropped`,
   `--allow-loss list-style-dropped`,
   `--allow-loss list-delimiter-changed`,
   `--allow-loss table-span-flattened`,
   `--allow-loss table-header-row-demoted`,
   `--allow-loss table-header-row-hoisted`,
   `--allow-loss table-header-cell-demoted`,
   `--allow-loss markdown-container-flattened`,
   `--allow-loss markdown-caption-flattened`,
   `--allow-loss fence-title-dropped`,
   `--allow-loss markdown-attributes-dropped` and
   `--allow-loss raw-html-escaped`. The list is closed:
   `destination-denied` is not waivable, because a blanked destination is a
   policy refusal, not a loss the target cannot carry: CARVE-P9-054 applies
   the denylist in both safe modes, and the path that keeps a denied
   destination lies outside the checked render (the Carve and AST exits, or
   an engine API option). Import-loss reports
   are separate: they describe source-to-AST migration, while this report
   describes AST-to-target rendering.
   ============================================================================ *)
